## std/net/http_route — algebraic effect for HTTP route registration.
##
## Operation Piezoelectric Effects, Phase 6.1.d. Declares the `HttpRoute`
## effect plus the v1 verb performers (`get`, `post`, `put`, `delete`,
## `patch`) that enable Sinatra-shaped block-DSL routing:
##
##   import std/net/http_server
##
##   def main(): Int
##     http_serve(8080) do ->
##       get("/", req: Request -> text_response("Hello"))
##       post("/users", req: Request -> json_response(req.body))
##     end
##     return 0
##   end
##
## `http_serve(port, body)` (declared in std/net/http_server) opens a
## `with HttpRoute { register: <captured> } do -> body() end` install
## before forwarding to the lower-level kqueue-loop server. Inside the
## body, every verb performer (`get`/`post`/...) calls `register(method,
## pattern, handler_int)` — an effect op whose default install captures
## the registration into the active server's router. Custom user handlers
## can intercept registrations for inspection, transformation, or
## test-time route mocking.
##
## Handler ABI: `def h(op_name: Int, argc: Int, argv: Int): Int`. argv[0]
## is the method (decode via `as_string`), argv[1] is the pattern
## (decode via `as_string`), argv[2] is the raw handler function pointer
## (use `call_fn(load(argv, 2), req)` to invoke; handles both plain fn
## pointers and tagged closures).
##
## Why `handler: Int` (raw fn ptr) instead of `Fn(Request): Response` in
## the effect declaration: the runtime invocation path passes args
## through an i64 argv array (existential erasure); the destination
## handler reads each slot via `load(argv, i)` and the only intrinsic
## from Int back to a typed function value is `call_fn`. Declaring the
## op's third arg as `Int` keeps the surface honest about what's
## actually flowing through the dispatcher. The verb performers convert
## `Fn(Request): Response` -> `Int` via `fn_to_int(handler)` at the
## perform site.

effect HttpRoute
  def register(method: String, pattern: String, handler: Int): Int
end
